┌───────────────────────────────────────┐
│ **Introduction**                        │
│                                           │
│ Uniform acceleration describes motion   │
│ in which the velocity of an object       │
│ changes at a constant rate. This means   │
│ the acceleration vector is constant in   │
│ magnitude and direction.                 │
└───────────────────────────────────────┘


┌───────────────────────────────────────┐
│ **Definition**                          │
│                                           │
│ *Uniform acceleration* is the condition  │
│ where the change in velocity over time   │
│ is steady, i.e., Δv/Δt is the same at     │
│ every instant.                           │
└───────────────────────────────────────┘


┌───────────────────────────────────────┐
│ **Key Concepts**                        │
│                                           │
│ • Acceleration (a) is a vector.         │
│ • Δv (change in velocity) can be        │
│   positive (speeding up) or negative     │
│   (slowing down).                       │
│ • Time interval (Δt) is the duration over │
│   which the change occurs.               │
│ • For uniform acceleration, a is        │
│   constant.                              │
└───────────────────────────────────────┘


┌───────────────────────────────────────┐
│ **Main Formula**                        │
│                                           │
│          a = Δv / Δt                    │
│                                           │
│ [ Explain Formula ]                      │
│ [ Open Formula Lab ]                     │
└───────────────────────────────────────┘


┌───────────────────────────────────────┐
│ **Summary**                             │
│                                           │
│ In uniform acceleration, the velocity   │
│ changes linearly with time. The simple   │
│ relation a = Δv/Δt lets us compute the   │
│ acceleration when we know the change in │
│ velocity over a known time interval.     │
└───────────────────────────────────────┘


